@ebarkhordar Thanks, that's right, and I could reproduce it the same way: a meta-init `BatchNorm1d` child with no `norm.` key in the state_dict stays on meta on 404f8172 and not on the merge base.

Applied your edit in 292bed80 in both places (`replace_module._replace_module` and `AutoTP._replace_module`): when an admitted module's prefix is missing, load its buffers before the `continue`. That is what the merge base already did for BatchNorm, since it went down the buffer path unadmitted.

Added `test_admitted_norm_absent_from_the_state_dict_still_gets_its_buffers_off_meta`, which drives `_replace_module` the way you did. It fails on 404f8172 and passes now; `test_autotp_custom_patterns.py` is 10 passed / 20 skipped on CPU. The test only covers the `replace_module` call site; the `AutoTP` one got the same three lines.

@ebarkhordar Right, turning a loud meta failure into a plausible wrong number is worse than what I was fixing. I reproduced it: on 292bed80, a meta `Phi3RotaryEmbedding` with a non-persistent `inv_freq` and no key in the shard comes back off meta.

Your gate is now in both call sites (121cd03). I added `test_non_persistent_buffers_of_an_absent_module_stay_on_meta` next to the BatchNorm test. It fails on 292bed80 and passes now, and the BatchNorm test still passes. `test_autotp_custom_patterns.py` is 11 passed / 20 skipped on CPU.

The gate doesn't cover a module that has both persistent and non-persistent buffers: its non-persistent ones would still be materialized. The in-shard path just below already does that today for any module with buffers, so I left it out of this PR.
